# The image an OPERATOR runs (CFV1-RUN, PDR-0002).
#
# It is deliberately a second file rather than a stage or a flag on the first
# one. `Dockerfile` is the development and CI container: its `CMD` is
# `npm run quality`, it installs git so the CFV1-BASE proofs can build real
# repositories, and it exists to prove the gate runs. Handing that image a
# different command at `docker run` would have "worked", and is exactly how an
# image whose default command is a test suite ends up in front of a user's
# library. The two images have different jobs, so they have different files, and
# `run/the-runtime-image-runs-the-process` reads both and fails if either starts
# behaving like the other.
FROM node:26-slim

WORKDIR /app

# No git here. The runtime never builds a repository — that is the gate's need,
# and a package the process does not use is a package an operator has to patch.

COPY package.json package-lock.json ./

# `npm ci` installs devDependencies too, and that is a deliberate cost rather
# than an oversight: the start command runs the TypeScript sources through
# `tsx`, so the toolchain has to be present. Compiling to JavaScript first and
# shipping a production-only install is the smaller image and the better answer
# eventually; it is a build pipeline this unit does not need in order to make the
# instance runnable, and adding one here would widen a unit whose subject is the
# process, not the packaging. What the choice costs is written here rather than
# discovered: a larger image, and a dev toolchain present at runtime.
RUN npm ci

COPY . .

# Configuration arrives through the environment at run time and this image
# carries none of it (see .env.example, SECURITY.md). The process refuses to
# start when a value is missing, naming it — `src/server/config.ts`.
#
# DATABASE_URL is required too, and is refused by the store's own seam rather
# than by that list (`src/persistence/configuration.ts`). The schema is NOT
# created by this image: an operator applies every file in `migrations/` once, in
# filename order, and a container started against a database missing any of them
# exits naming the table it could not read instead of serving 500s (ADR-0027).
#
# PORT is not defaulted here for the same reason it is not defaulted in the
# code: an instance that binds a port nobody chose is an instance whose operator
# finds out from a proxy that cannot reach it.
EXPOSE 8080

# The one thing this image does, and it is the declared start command's own
# argv rather than `npm start`.
#
# Measured on this branch: with `CMD ["npm", "start"]` the container answered
# every request correctly and then FAILED to shut down. `docker stop` sends
# SIGTERM to PID 1, PID 1 was npm, npm died, and the process that installed the
# shutdown handler never saw the signal — so the drain never ran and the
# container was killed after the timeout instead of stopping. The instance's
# whole operating model is being stopped as soon as it is idle, so a stop that
# does not reach the handler is not a detail.
#
# `node --import tsx` is ONE process (the tsx CLI would spawn a child and put
# the same hop back). `tests/run/containment.test.ts` compares this argv with
# the `start` script in `package.json`, so the two cannot drift; the local proof
# `run/the-process-serves-and-stops` splits and runs that same declared command
# for the same reason.
CMD ["node", "--import", "tsx", "src/server/main.ts"]
